    "summary": "Strong set with epic The Other One and heartfelt Comes A Time",
    "blurb": "The show features an extended Bertha, a soaring The Other One suite, and a moving Comes A Time finale. Jerry nods into the ballad at just the right moment while the rhythm section stays tight. Recording quality is fair due to intermittent Keith piano distortion.",
    "review": "On November 6 1971 the Dead delivered a solid two‑set performance at the Harding Theater that still feels fresh for listeners today. Set 1 opens with an energetic Bertha that rolls straight into classic road‑trip fare – Me And My Uncle, Sugaree and Jack Straw – before winding down through a tight run of country‑rock numbers like Tennessee Jed and Loser. The second set is where the magic really lifts off: after a brief Ramble On Rose, the band dives into the Cryptical Envelopment → The Other One suite, threading cryptic passages into a powerful jam that showcases both the group's improvisational depth and tight interplay. The night closes on a beautifully understated Comes A Time, with Jerry giving the perfect nod to the song’s gentle mood.\n\nThe sole soundboard source captures the band cleanly for the most part, though Keith’s piano channel suffers from intermittent distortion that can be distracting, especially on disc 3. Aside from that hiccup the mix is clear and the performance shines – a noteworthy snapshot of the early‑’70s Dead before Mickey Hart rejoined.\n\nFor anyone interested in hearing this era’s blend of tight songcraft and exploratory jamming, this recording is the go‑to version despite its minor keyboard flaws.",
